WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.450
Speaker 1: I want to go back because we're
talking about economics.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:10.040
I want to go back to something I've been telling
you about for a decade at this point in time.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:17.759
There are now more sort of mainstream media
outlets taking this topic seriously.

00:00:17.759 --> 00:00:24.250
If you go back a really long time, certainly
50 years, you can go back 80.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:28.390
It starts to become less useful if you go
beyond when the parties switched.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:34.739
But if you go back 50 years when you have
a Democratic president in the White House,

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:36.840
job growth does better.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.929
People's income and income growth does better
and GDP growth does better.

00:00:41.929 --> 00:00:45.489
Now, as always, there is no one.

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:51.019
There are no two or three metrics which tell
you everything you need to know about how

00:00:51.019 --> 00:00:52.429
an economy is doing.

00:00:52.429 --> 00:00:55.469
You can say, but we need to look at wages,
labor participation, inflation, all these

00:00:55.469 --> 00:00:56.469
different things.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:00:57.969
Consumer confidence, sure.

00:00:57.969 --> 00:01:04.949
But jobs, income and GDP all taken together
are well-accepted metrics of how an economy

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:06.320
is doing.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.500
And they all do better on average when the
president is a Democrat.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:15.850
I am thrilled and we're linking to this article
that Newsweek is finally talking about this.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:22.280
They write nearly all major U.S. economic
indicators, including income productivity,

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.619
stock prices, jobs and domestic product.

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:32.140
Show growth under democratic presidents compared
to when Republicans are in the White House.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:36.159
I'm not going to give you all the data again
because we've given it to you so many times.

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:42.900
If you had the choice going back between saying,
Do I want to be in the job market when there's

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.180
a Democrat in the White House or a Republican?

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.909
Statistically, you would have been better
off when there's a Democrat in the White House

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:54.470
if you had said, I'm going to invest in the
S&amp;P 500 when there's a Democrat in the White

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:55.740
House or a Republican.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.320
If you could pick only the years that one
or the other is in the White House, your investment

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:06.130
return would be dramatically better if you
had invested only in the Democratic years.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:10.550
Now, remember that stocks in general tend
to go up, so you would have done OK in either

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:15.530
case, but you would have done better when
there was a Democratic president in the White

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:16.530
House.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:22.030
The New York Times released an analysis on
this, and the economy does better when Democrats

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.580
are president by every single metric.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:32.000
Now, whenever this is presented, whenever
this fact is presented, and it is a fact.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.240
You might not like it.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:38.370
You might want to explain it away, but it
is a fact we hear many arguments against one

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:42.950
of the arguments we hear is, David, you're
looking at the wrong thing.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:50.950
Those metrics did better because it was a
Republican controlled House or a Republican

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.370
controlled Senate or both.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:03:00.060
And the answer is no, there is no clear Partizan
pattern.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.550
When you look at control of the house and
control of the Senate, other than it sometimes

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:10.150
matches what is going on in the Oval Office.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.150
That's not a good argument.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.740
Next argument that's often made is Democrats
just get lucky.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.650
You know what happens?

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:23.050
Republicans have these recessions and they
fix them, and then they leave office.

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:28.300
And then in comes a Democratic president,
and they get all the credit for what a Republican

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.300
did.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:30.540
And there's two answers to that.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:37.230
The first answer to that is, OK, let's pretend
that what you're saying is true.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:45.420
Let's give the first six months of any one
president's economic growth to the last guy.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:50.680
So in other words, what that would mean is
Barack Obama's first six months will give

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:57.320
to George W. Bush, give George W. Bush a little
bit of the credit for the Obama recovery.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.660
It's still favors Democrats.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:03.040
So even if you kind of put in place a six
month grace period, the numbers still don't

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:05.380
look much better for Republicans.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:11.340
But maybe the most important thing about this
is if it's true that Democrats are coming

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.810
in and experiencing economic recoveries when
they are in the White House, why are there

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:21.900
so many Republican recessions that have to
be fixed during Democratic administrations?

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.259
So the analysis is extraordinarily clear.

00:04:25.259 --> 00:04:27.090
It's a well-known phenomenon.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:32.240
And yet this is why you know, we opened the
show today by saying.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.069
Economic reality.

00:04:34.069 --> 00:04:37.949
Political reality, you might have an economic
reality.

00:04:37.949 --> 00:04:41.539
Politically, it doesn't exist unless people
believe it.

00:04:41.539 --> 00:04:43.669
And much the same way there's this.

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:46.330
It's really a meme at this point, it's a selfie.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:47.669
It almost spreads virally.

00:04:47.669 --> 00:04:49.710
There's this idea.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:53.039
That Republicans are better stewards of the
economy.

00:04:53.039 --> 00:05:03.060
There are no statistical sets of data that
you can point to in which that is the case.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.590
Let me know via Twitter.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:06.620
You can find me at Packman.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:13.770
Why does this talking point about Republicans
being good for the economy persist so strongly?

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:15.479
Because it does find me on Twitter.

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:16.479
Let me know.

